8.3  Event loops: one thread, thousands of connections

TL;DR
  - A thread per connection spends most of its time waiting. An event loop asks
    the kernel "tell me when any of these sockets is ready", then handles
    whichever is, one at a time.
  - On Linux that question is epoll. Node.js, nginx, Redis, Python's asyncio and
    Rust's tokio are built on it.
  - One blocking call (a slow file read, a CPU-heavy loop) freezes every
    connection the loop serves.

      strace -f -e trace=epoll_wait,epoll_ctl -p PID   # the loop at work

On your machine
  Files     1,024 open per process, can be raised to 524,288
  Processes 62,844 for your user, threads included
  Stack     8.0 MiB per thread, can be raised without limit
  System    14,208 files open on the whole system

Pitfalls
  - Run CPU-heavy work or blocking libraries on a worker thread or process;
    async frameworks have helpers for it (run_in_executor, spawn_blocking).
  - Every connection is a file descriptor: the open-files limit caps how many
    you can hold (8.4).
  - Regular files are always "ready" for epoll: disk reads still block, which is
    why runtimes use threads, or io_uring, for files.

Deep dive

  ▾ How does epoll scale?

    The older select and poll take the whole list of descriptors on every call,
    so the cost grows with the number of connections. With epoll, the program
    registers each descriptor once; the kernel keeps a ready list filled by the
    network stack, and epoll_wait returns only the descriptors that have
    something to do. Ten thousand idle connections cost nothing.

  ▾ What does io_uring change?

    Instead of asking "is it ready?" and then calling read, a program puts whole
    requests in a ring shared with the kernel and collects results from another
    ring: fewer system calls, and real asynchronous file I/O. It's newer and has
    had security issues, so some distros and containers turn it off.

